.TH MODBUS-CACHE 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.4.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-cache \- Modbus TCP caching proxy for single-client devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-cache
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B modbus-cache \-\-dry-run
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmodbus-cache\fR owns the single Modbus TCP client connection a device
allows (e.g. a Huawei SUN2000 inverter and its SDongle/EMMA radio accept
exactly one) and multiplexes it: a background loop polls the configured
register ranges into an in-memory cache, any number of clients are served
FC3/FC4 reads from the cache, reads outside the map and other function
codes are forwarded live, and writes (FC05/06/15/16/22/23) are forwarded
when explicitly enabled and audited \(em gated by a register allow-list
(\fB--write-registers\fR) that spells its ranges directly, takes the
device profile's \fBacl\fR/\fBwrite\fR gate (the ordered rule chain:
one \fBtype\fR allow/deny and a \fBrange\fR per rule \(em the last
matching rule decides, a register no rule decides refuses, the word
\fBany\fR = all registers), or reads a standalone acl file
(\fBfile:<path>\fR \(em the same gate object a profile carries under
acl/write, compiled at start and on every set). Every function code crossing the
wire is named in the log and the audit trail (all standard FCs, FC1
through FC43). A synthesized diagnostics block at registers
65000\(en65003 lets clients tell cached data from live data.
.PP
Modbus auth (\fB--auth\fR): with \fBsuite\fR the cache's own clients
prove a shared key (\fB--auth-key\fR, min 16 characters) with a crypt
handshake right after every connect \(em 52 bytes: magic, fresh nonce,
HMAC\-SHA256 proof; the cache answers \fBOK\fR (plain Modbus follows)
or \fBERR\fR (the client is dropped before it ever sees a frame).
\fB--auth-upstream\fR/\fB--auth-upstream-key\fR do the same toward
the inverter: an \fBERR\fR fails the exchange like any other upstream
fault and the poller retries. The upstream leg also speaks
\fB--auth-upstream waveshare\fR for a Waveshare RS485\-to\-TCP
gateway: the shared key goes out as the gateway's connection password
(raw ASCII, right after every connect) and the gateway's \fBOK\fR opens
the plain Modbus session \(em anything else fails the exchange like any
other upstream fault. Empty auth = no auth at all.
.PP
Resilience: when the device dies, cached reads keep serving last-known
values and forwarded reads get exception 0x0B; when the device rejects a
range, the poll status is remembered instead of hammering it; garbage from
either side is answered one line at a time and never fatal. Verified
against the fake inverter, fuzzing and stress suites \-\- not against real
hardware.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-upstream
the inverter Modbus TCP endpoint HOST:PORT (`:5502` = 127.0.0.1:5502)
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " N"
Modbus unit id
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " N"
Modbus connect/read timeout in seconds
.TP
.B \-\-auth
the auth scheme the cache's own Modbus clients must speak: `suite`
demands the crypt handshake (magic + nonce + HMAC proof of the shared
key) right after every connect and drops the client on ERR/anything
else; none/empty = no auth at all
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-key
the shared key the cache's clients prove with the suite handshake (min
16 characters); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-upstream
the auth scheme toward the upstream inverter: `suite` proves the shared
auth\-upstream\-key with a crypt handshake right after every connect
(the peer answers OK, or ERR and the connection fails — the poller keeps
retrying); `waveshare` sends the shared auth\-upstream\-key as the
Waveshare TCP connection password right after every connect (the gateway
answers OK, or anything else/nothing and the connection fails — the
poller keeps retrying); none/empty = no auth at all
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-upstream\-key
the key the cache presents to the upstream inverter — the suite
handshake's shared key (min 16 characters) or the Waveshare TCP
connection password (non\-empty ASCII, the gateway's own rule); the
value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file
(a systemd LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " N"
seconds between cache polls
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-delay " N"
seconds between consecutive inverter reads within a poll cycle, 0 = no
delay
.TP
.BI \-\-merge\-gap " N"
max register gap when merging ranges into block reads, 0 = read ranges
separately
.TP
.BI \-\-cache\-ttl " N"
per\-register cache TTL: cached registers older than this are refetched
from the inverter, 0 = never expire, serve last known values forever
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-write
forward write requests (FC05/06/15/16/22/23) to the inverter; without it
they refuse with a Modbus illegal\-function exception
.TP
.B \-\-write\-registers
address ranges a (forwarded) write may touch — the register writes
(FC06/16/22/23) and the coil writes (FC05/15, the coil space) are gated
by the same ranges: comma\-separated START:COUNT, e.g.
47400:100,47600:100; "any" = unrestricted; "none" = nothing writable;
"profile" = the device profile's write acl ("acl": {"write": {"rules":
[{"type": "allow"|"deny", "range": "START:COUNT"}]}} — a whitelist: the
last matching rule decides, a register no rule decides refuses);
"file:<path>" = the standalone acl file (the same gate object a profile
carries under "acl"/"write", read and compiled at start and on every
set)
.TP
.B \-\-profile
device profile JSON (the shared profiles/ schema)
.TP
.B \-\-selection
selection JSON: {"registers": ["pv\-power",...]} — the profile names to
fetch
.TP
.B \-\-connect\-acl
clients allowed to connect: "any", "none" (refuse everyone),
comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like 10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16, or
"file:<path>" — the standalone ip\-prefix acl file (the gate object
{"type": "ip\-prefix", "rules": [{"type": "allow"|"deny", "prefix":
\&...}]}: the last matching rule decides, a client no rule decides
refuses; "any" = 0.0.0.0/0, the whole space)
.TP
.B \-\-write\-acl
clients allowed to write (requires \-\-allow\-write): "any", "none"
(refuse everyone), comma\-separated IPs/prefixes, or "file:<path>" — the
standalone ip\-prefix acl file (the same gate object acl/connect reads:
the last matching rule decides, a client no rule decides refuses)
.TP
.B \-\-listen
listen address HOST:PORT
.TP
.B \-\-local\-time
log timestamps in the system time zone instead of UTC
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
refuse further connections past this many simultaneous clients, 0 = no
limit
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " N"
disconnect clients silent for this many seconds, 0 = never
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
refuse further connections from one peer IP past this many simultaneous
connections, 0 = no per\-IP limit
.TP
.B \-\-control\-port
control server target: unix socket path or TCP port on 127.0.0.1; 0 =
off
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token":...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer (root,
the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED) skips
the token; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run the show/clear verbs only (no set/unset, no request dump/restore, no
restart, no shutdown) — the ops shift against the admin key, both
audited; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-verbs
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated
.TP
.B \-\-control\-trust\-uids
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the plain 0600) ($CONTROL_SOCKET_GROUP)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-server
serve the cache's own Prometheus /metrics endpoint (the live `set
metrics enable` toggle exists too)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-listen
the metrics bind address HOST:PORT — 0.0.0.0:PORT serves every
interface, a token is mandatory beyond loopback (a token\-less
non\-loopback bind refuses to start, F13)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on the /metrics endpoint (the same
scheme the scraper speaks); the value may be `file:<path>` — the first
line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-acl
clients allowed to connect to the /metrics endpoint: "any", "none"
(refuse everyone), comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like
10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16, or "file:<path>" — the standalone ip\-prefix acl
file (the gate object {"type": "ip\-prefix", "rules": [{"type":
"allow"|"deny", "prefix": ...}]}: the last matching rule decides, a
client no rule decides refuses; "any" = 0.0.0.0/0, the whole space);
checked at accept — a client off the ACL is closed without a reply
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients on the /metrics endpoint; extras get an
immediate 503, 0 = unlimited
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle /metrics client is kept before the socket times out, 0 =
the built\-in 5 s cap
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent /metrics connections from one peer IP, 0 = no per\-IP
limit
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-log
CACHE audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald
way); $AUDIT_LOG or ~/.local/state/modbus\-cache/audit.log — the XDG
state home
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " N"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping N backups; 0 = no size
rotation
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-daily
also rotate the audit log at UTC midnight
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " N"
rotated audit backups to keep
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the CACHE audit trail
.TP
.BI \-\-requests\-audit " N"
audit one REQUESTS tally per client per this many seconds, 0 = off
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level
log level
.TP
.B \-\-log\-access
log client connects/disconnects at INFO instead of DEBUG
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-path
the state dump (write\-behind): the live setting overrides and — with
\-\-dump\-with\-cache — the cached words, as one JSON, written 0600 with
an integrity hash (a restore refuses a tampered, unsigned or
group\-writable dump); dumped on shutdown, on the request dump command
and every \-\-dump\-interval seconds; loaded on start only with
\-\-restore\-on\-start, on demand with the request restore command
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-key
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
words and the settings ride encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode
keystream, authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and
a configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash; a dump path
without a key warns at start and on set; the value may be `file:<path>`
— the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential
rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-with\-cache
dump the cached words too: `request dump` and the \-\-dump\-interval
loop write the words + the settings (`request dump with\-cache` always
includes them; the restore takes either shape) ($DUMP_WITH_CACHE)
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-interval " N"
seconds between the automatic state dumps, 0 = on request (the request
dump command and the shutdown)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the daemon starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — a
restart serves last\-known values instead of cold misses; without it the
dump loads on demand ($RESTORE_ON_START)
.TP
.B \-\-config
config file — the set grammar: every line a `set <name> <value>`,
comments start with # ($CONFIG, then modbus_cache.conf next to this
script, then the subproject root)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration and register map, print the effective
settings and exit (nothing is bound or connected)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
reload the configuration and the register map without dropping clients
(the file always wins over live settings; a broken file keeps the previous
state). The listen address and the log level need a restart.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
clean the cache: all cached values, on-demand entries and poll-status
errors are dropped; the poller repopulates immediately.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown.

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines: one request
\fB{"cmd": "..."}\fR per line, one reply
\fB{"status": "ok|fail|bye", "text": "..."}\fR per line. Commands
(the settings registry drives the grammar):
.BR set ", " unset ", " show ", " help ,
the reports
.BR inspect ", " "show connections" ,
.BR "show cache" " [all]" ", " "show write registers profile" ,
.BR "show uplink" ", " "show version" ,
.BR "show acl connect table" ", " "show acl write table" ,
.BR "show metrics acl table" ,
the ops
.BR "request dump" ", " "request restore" ", " "request clear cache" ,
.BR "request reload" ", " "request restart" ", " shutdown ", " quit .
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B set <setting> <value>
changes a setting live (modbus interval, cache ttl, modbus read-delay,
modbus merge-gap, write allow, write registers, limits max-clients,
limits client-timeout, modbus upstream, modbus unit, metrics enable,
metrics listen, the metrics limits caps, metrics acl,
audit requests, dump-interval, log-level, local-time, modbus profile,
modbus selection, acl connect, acl write, modbus timeout, modbus auth,
modbus auth-upstream, control port, control ops verbs, control
trust-uids, control socket-group, audit log-file, audit rotate max-size,
audit disable, dump path, dump with-cache); a SIGHUP re-applies the config
(the file always wins). A knob the registry marks
.B restart: true
answers the note
.B restart required (startup-only knob)
from set/unset \-\- the set records the value (show renders it; the
whole-daemon reload folds it into the fresh process before the binds),
the config (and a SIGHUP) changes it too. Every secret in the registry
is restart-marked (the tokens, the auth keys, dump/key) and refuses
set/unset outright: a secret never rides the reload state (a secret knob
refuses to show at all \-\- the value renders nowhere) \-\- it passes
by flag/env/config and a restart.
.IP \(bu 2
.B show cache all
prints every cached entry: name, words, values, age, expiry, poll status.
.IP \(bu 2
.B show uplink
analyses the inverter link: session counters (connects, exchanges, bytes,
errors) and one row per poll block with its status, latency (last/mean/max)
and last error.
.IP \(bu 2
.B show write registers profile
renders the write gate as its acl chain (show-only): \fBallow any\fR /
\fBdeny any\fR, a range list as its whitelist (deny any, then one allow
line per range), and in profile or file mode the gate's own rules \(em
the gate's desc as the % line, then one allow/deny line per range with
its desc, the comments aligned.
.IP \(bu 2
.B show acl connect table
/ \fBshow acl write table\fR / \fBshow metrics acl table\fR render the
IP ACL as its chain, one
entry per line (show-only): \fBallow 0.0.0.0/0\fR (any), \fBdeny
0.0.0.0/0\fR (none), \fBdeny 0.0.0.0/0\fR then one \fBallow\fR line
per network (the whitelist) \(em and in file mode the ip-prefix gate's
own rules, the gate desc as the % line, the rule descs as aligned
comments.
.IP \(bu 2
.B request dump
/ \fBrequest restore\fR: the write-behind state (the cached words + stamps,
the live setting overrides) to/from the \fB--dump-path\fR file; the startup
restore and the shutdown dump run the same path automatically. An overridden
secret rides as \fB*** SECRET REMOVED ***\fR \(em the knob renders, the
value never lands in the file, the restore skips the placeholder and names
it. With \fB--dump-key\fR the dump is sealed: the state rides encrypted
(an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode keystream, authenticated \(em a restore
refuses a wrong or missing key). The dump
commands \(em and the \fBdump-interval\fR setting \(em show up in the
listings only when a dump path is configured.
.IP \(bu 2
.B request clear cache
/ SIGUSR1, \fBrequest restart\fR (drop the upstream + the cache, repoll),
\fBrequest reload\fR (save the live overrides to a state file and re-exec the
whole daemon \(em the fresh process re-resolves the config, so the
restart-marked knobs take their configured values, and the saved
overrides re-apply once; the cache starts empty),
\fBshutdown\fR (the same as SIGTERM).
.RE
.PP
See
.BR modbus-cache-ctl (1)
and
.BR modbus-cache-cli (1)
for the clients.

.SH SECURITY
The control surface is loopback-only (TCP) or a 0600 unix socket; a TCP
control port without a token refuses to start (F5); with a token the
wire is signed and the commands/replies verify end to end; a second,
read-only ops token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) authenticates sessions
into the show/clear verbs only \(em no set/unset, no request
dump/restore, no request reload, no request restart, no shutdown \(em
the ops shift against the admin key, both roles audited. A secret knob
(control token, the ops token, the metrics token, modbus auth key, dump
key) never shows its value: \fBshow\fR answers \fBerr <name> is a
secret \(em it never shows\fR, \fBunset\fR refuses \(em pass the
secret by flag/env, or as \fBfile:<path>\fR; \fBshow config\fR and the
dumps render the knob as \fB*** SECRET REMOVED ***\fR (the config
parser refuses the placeholder \(em it is never a value).
.PP
The Modbus port is read-only until the write gate says otherwise:
writes ride \fB--write-registers\fR (the direct ranges, the profile's
acl/write chain or a \fBfile:<path>\fR acl) and every accepted or
refused write lands in the audit trail (WRITE / WRITE\-REFUSED). The
\fB--connect-acl\fR IP gate (acl/connect) filters who may connect at
all; the metrics endpoint carries the family's one /metrics stack (F13):
a token-less non-loopback bind refuses to start, the metrics acl IP
gate narrows who reaches the endpoint, the caps bound the concurrency.
With \fB--dump-key\fR the state file is sealed (an HMAC\-SHA256
counter\-mode keystream, authenticated \(em a restore refuses a wrong
or missing key). The audit trail (in the XDG state home when
\fB--audit-log\fR is unset, prefix CACHE) records the control commands
with their roles, the auth failures, the writes and the reloads.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTL
control target for the daemon's clients when the flag is unset (flag
\fB--control-port\fR wins).
.TP
.B HOST ", " LISTEN ", " ALLOW_WRITE ", " ...
every flag has an environment twin of the same bare name (no tool
prefix); the flag wins over the env, the env over the config script.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/modbus-cache-<port>.socket
the control socket used when \fB--control-port\fR is unset (stale files
replaced, unlinked on shutdown).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/modbus-cache/audit.log
the audit trail when \fB--audit-log\fR is unset.
.TP
.I cache/examples/modbus_cache.conf ", " cache/examples/selection.json ,
.I examples/acl-write-registers.json
the packaged sample config, the example selection and the example acl
file at the repo root (the deye write policy for \fB--write-registers
file:<path>\fR \(em
a group/world-readable acl file warns in the log, the audit trail
(ACL-FILE) and on the set/unset reply; chmod 0600 silences it).

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (\fB--dry-run\fR: valid configuration).
.TP
.B 1
invalid configuration or a failed bind (reason on stderr).
.TP
.B 2
command-line misuse.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-cache-ctl (1),
.BR modbus-cache-cli (1),
.BR modbus-scraper (1),
.BR modbus-fake-inverter (1)
.PP
